1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,460
Good afternoon. Can I help you?

2
00:00:02,460 --> 00:00:07,427
Good afternoon. Yes, I've just transferred to the School of Education,

3
00:00:07,427 --> 00:00:11,040
and I'd like information about joining the library.

4
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:16,088
Well, the School of Education has libraries on two sites, as I'm sure you know.

5
00:00:16,088 --> 00:00:20,800
This one here is the Fordham Site, and the other is on Castle Road.

6
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:24,850
And is there any difference between the two libraries?

7
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:28,950
Not in terms of their facilities. Access to online databases

8
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:32,380
and the Internet is available at both sites

9
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:36,740
and each site has a range of reference materials on education.

10
00:00:36,740 --> 00:00:38,690
Oh yes. I see.

11
00:00:38,690 --> 00:00:43,480
But the Castle Road site has books on the sociology of education

12
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:45,912
and a collection of textbooks

13
00:00:45,912 --> 00:00:51,010
and teaching resources covering most of the subjects taught in secondary schools.

14
00:00:51,010 --> 00:00:54,806
Ah, right, but I'm training to be a primary teacher

15
00:00:54,806 --> 00:01:00,120
so I need to look at materials for the five to eleven age group.

16
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:02,545
Then you've come to the right place.

17
00:01:02,545 --> 00:01:07,395
At Fordham we hold material relating to primary education, as well as special needs,

18
00:01:07,395 --> 00:01:13,630
but of course you'll need to familiarise yourself with both sites to make the most of our resources.

19
00:01:13,630 --> 00:01:19,530
You haven't mentioned periodicals. Are they held at both sites?

20
00:01:19,530 --> 00:01:23,360
Current issues, yes but if you want to look at back issues

21
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:25,587
you'll need to use the CD-ROM databases

22
00:01:25,587 --> 00:01:27,500
which are held here at Fordham.

23
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:31,790
I see. Now, about borrowing books. I'm living out of town,

24
00:01:31,790 --> 00:01:36,880
so I'm hoping I can borrow quite a few items

25
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:40,370
and cut down on the number of trips I have to make.

26
00:01:40,370 --> 00:01:44,630
Right, well, members can borrow two books at a time from each site.

27
00:01:44,630 --> 00:01:46,370
Only two books?

28
00:01:46,370 --> 00:01:49,830
Only two from each site, but that's four altogether.

29
00:01:49,830 --> 00:01:53,770
Oh I see, and how long can I hang on to them for?

30
00:01:53,770 --> 00:01:58,422
The borrowing period is one month, but of course books can be renewed.

31
00:01:58,422 --> 00:02:02,000
You can renew any item a maximum of three times.

32
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,820
Do I have to come to the library to do that?

33
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:07,944
No, you can do it by telephone or email,

34
00:02:07,944 --> 00:02:10,720
but you can't renew overdue books this way

35
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:14,538
only before or on the due date stamped in the book.

36
00:02:14,538 --> 00:02:16,520
We'll need your full name

37
00:02:16,820 --> 00:02:21,480
your borrower number and the name of the site library you borrowed the items from.

38
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:26,040
So theoretically I can borrow books for up to three months

39
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:27,820
Is that what you're saying?

40
00:02:27,820 --> 00:02:30,000
Yes, provided they're not recalled.

41
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,420
So, what happens then?

42
00:02:32,420 --> 00:02:35,454
Well, sometimes an item is requested by another borrower,

43
00:02:35,454 --> 00:02:37,880
in which case we'd send you a letter

44
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:41,858
and you'd have to return the book within seven working days.

45
00:02:41,858 --> 00:02:43,880
Don't forget we're closed on Sundays.

46
00:02:43,880 --> 00:02:44,660
OK.

47
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:50,970
I expect it'll take me a while to find what I need. There's such a lot here.

48
00:02:50,970 --> 00:02:54,200
Yes there is, but if you need help getting started

49
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:57,820
this term we're running three study skills workshops.

50
00:02:57,820 --> 00:03:00,130
Oh? What are they on?

51
00:03:00,130 --> 00:03:05,791
Er, let me see. The first one's on resources-yes, here it is.

52
00:03:05,791 --> 00:03:09,180
How to use the library's resources

53
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:13,340
That includes everything, not just the print and technical resources.

54
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:17,800
That sounds useful. Is there anything on using the Internet?

55
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:24,281
Er, let's see. The one on the Internet for beginners was last term.

56
00:03:24,281 --> 00:03:27,770
This term it's finding research materials online.

57
00:03:27,770 --> 00:03:32,760
That sounds interesting too, hm, what's the last one?

58
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:35,420
It's a workshop on dissertations.

59
00:03:35,420 --> 00:03:38,800
What do you mean. How to write one?

60
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:42,486
Er, no it's more to do with academic writing conventions,

61
00:03:42,486 --> 00:03:47,278
you know-writing a bibliography and how to refer to sources in your text.

62
00:03:47,278 --> 00:03:52,070
That sort of thing. Anyway, here's a leaflet with information about all three.

63
00:03:52,070 --> 00:03:55,260
Thanks a lot. That'll be very useful.

64
00:03:55,260 --> 00:03:55,680
You're welcome.
